insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 1, 'Two rows, x + y <= 2 and x + y >= 3, sit under the objective minimise x + y. Which answer comes back from solveLP?', '["optimal, objective 3.0000, where x + y >= 3 holds", "unbounded, since x + y has no floor", "infeasible, since no point meets both rows", "it throws, as the rows contradict each other"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Infeasible and unbounded are answers, and the digest prints this problem''s status as infeasible. The kernel throws only on a malformed problem.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 2, 'In the kernel''s own terms, what is the 400 bbl of Butane the Apapa terminal holds?', '["A bound on one variable, the upper limit on Butane''s volume.", "A row, because it limits how the volumes of the four components behave jointly.", "A coefficient of the objective, since the tank volume enters the cost of the recipe.", "The batch row''s right-hand side, the barrels to deliver."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The bounds are passed apart from the rows: the kernel shifts every lower bound to the origin, and it gives a bound no shadow price. A tank limit belongs to one component, so it is a bound on that one variable, and a component held by it reports as a component at its availability.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 3, 'A planner asks why the Sulfur maximum cannot be entered as a bound on the kernel. What is the reason?', '["A bound can only hold a whole number, and the sulfur limit is carried as a ratio of two sums over the components.", "The kernel accepts bounds of zero only, so every tank limit and specification has to be written as a row.", "Bounds are minimised with the objective, so a sulfur bound would be driven to zero.", "A specification constrains a mixture of volumes, and a bound belongs to one volume alone."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A bound belongs to one variable. A specification limits a blended property of all the volumes together, so it is a row, and the bounds stay with each component''s floor and availability.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 4, 'The four corners of the textbook region print objectives 0.0000, 20.0000, 12.0000 and 21.0000. Where does the course say the optimum of a linear programme is found?', '["Inside the region, where no constraint holds and the kernel can move freely.", "At a vertex of the feasible region, here x 3.0000, y 1.5000.", "Midway along the edge joining the corners at 20.0000 and 21.0000.", "At the origin, where both bounds hold exactly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The optimum of a linear programme, when there is one, is found at a vertex of the feasible region. The kernel''s optimum for the textbook case is x 3.0000, y 1.5000, objective 21.0000, one of the four corners.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 5, 'What does solveLP promise its caller about the status of every problem it is posed?', '["A status of optimal, infeasible or unbounded, and the caller is told which.", "A recipe, with an infeasible problem answered by the nearest point that fits.", "An optimum, or an error thrown whenever the rows cannot all be met.", "A status of optimal or failed, with the reason left to the caller."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Its status is always one of optimal, infeasible or unbounded, and the caller is told which. Infeasible and unbounded are answers. A malformed problem is not an answer, and there the kernel throws.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 6, 'The Apapa pool blends four components. By the course''s definition of a vertex, how many constraints and bounds must hold exactly at its optimum?', '["Exactly two, one for each binding specification the recipe presses against.", "At least five, one for each specification in the 50 ppm gasoline template.", "At least eight, a lower and an upper bound for each of the four components.", "At least four, with the batch row counted among them."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A vertex is a point where at least as many constraints and bounds hold exactly as there are variables. The Apapa pool blends four components, so four volumes, and the batch row sum(v_i) = target is an equation met by every recipe.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 7, 'The kernel is asked to minimise x with a lower bound of 5 above an upper bound of 3. What does it return?', '["The status optimal, with x placed on its upper bound of 3.", "The status infeasible, because no value of x fits both of its bounds.", "The status unbounded, since nothing limits how small x may go.", "It throws, as for a row with the wrong number of coefficients."]'::jsonb, 1, 'No row is needed for this verdict. A single variable whose lower bound sits above its upper bound has no value that fits, so the problem has no feasible point, and infeasible is the answer.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 8, 'Maximise 3x + 2y with x + y <= 2 and y >= 2. Both rows press on y. What does the kernel return?', '["infeasible, because y >= 2 uses the whole allowance of the first row", "unbounded, because the second row sets no upper limit on y", "optimal, objective 4.0000", "optimal, objective 21.0000, the textbook case''s value"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A region that is very small is still a region. The kernel finds a feasible point and a best objective on it, 4.0000.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 9, 'A row is written with 1 coefficient on a problem with two variables, and the kernel stops with "Row 0 has 1 coefficients, expected 2". How is that result to be read?', '["As infeasible, since the short row contradicts the rest.", "As unbounded, since one variable is free of that row.", "As optimal on the one variable the short row names.", "As a fault in the question, with no status attached to it at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A malformed problem is not an answer, and the kernel throws. Any of the three statuses would be a claim about a problem nobody posed.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 10, 'In two-phase simplex, which phase finds that the rows contradict, and so returns infeasible?', '["Phase one, when it cannot drive every artificial variable to zero.", "Phase two, when no neighbouring vertex improves the objective any further.", "Phase two, when the pivot count passes a limit and stops.", "Neither: the kernel compares each row with the bounds first."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Driving the artificial variables to zero is how phase one finds a point meeting every row. When that fails, the rows contradict, and infeasible is the verdict.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 11, 'The textbook result prints iterations 2. What does that count?', '["The rows that hold exactly at the optimum, which in the textbook case number two.", "The phases the method ran, one to find a point and one to reach the optimum.", "The pivots of phase one and phase two together.", "The corners the kernel visited on its way, the origin and then the optimum."]'::jsonb, 2, 'iterations counts the pivots the kernel made, phase one and phase two together, and the textbook case took 2.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 12, 'Why does the kernel choose its entering variable by Bland''s rule on blending problems in particular?', '["Blends have more variables than rows, and Bland''s rule adds the missing rows.", "Blends are degenerate constantly, and Bland''s rule cannot cycle.", "Bland''s rule finds a start that a batch equation cannot give.", "Blending costs are positive, and Bland''s rule needs them to be."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Specifications bind exactly at the optimum, so blending problems are degenerate constantly. Bland''s rule picks the entering variable, and it cannot cycle.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 13, 'Maximise x with x - y <= 1, y unbounded above. What status does the kernel return?', '["infeasible, since a variable with no upper bound has no vertex", "optimal where x - y reaches 1 and the row holds exactly", "none: it throws on a variable with no upper bound", "unbounded, since x can grow with y and the objective has no ceiling"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The region is not empty and x can grow with y without end, so the objective has no ceiling. Unbounded is an answer the kernel reports as plainly as optimal and infeasible.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 14, 'The LP kernel uses absolute tolerances on its pivots and on phase one. What does the course state about where those tolerances are shown to hold?', '["Right at the barrel scale of the two apps'' cargoes; nothing in the course shows them on a problem scaled in millions.", "They are relative to each row''s largest coefficient, so every scale is handled alike.", "They are tuned for very large problems, and an 8000 bbl cargo falls below their resolution.", "They are shown to hold in phase two alone, where the pivots move toward the optimum."]'::jsonb, 0, 'L4 is held and taught as a limit. The course shows the tolerances working at barrel scale and makes no claim for coefficients in the millions.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-what-a-linear-programme-is', 15, 'A blend minimises cost, and the textbook case maximises earnings. What does that difference change about the method?', '["A maximisation is refused, since solveLP only minimises its objective.", "A minimisation needs its costs written as rows first.", "The same kernel does both: it minimises, or maximises when asked.", "A maximisation has no shadow prices to report."]'::jsonb, 2, 'solveLP minimises c''x subject to its rows and bounds, or maximises when asked. The textbook case is a maximisation and still carries shadow prices, 0.7500 and 0.5000.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 1, 'The Apapa AGO cargo of 6000 bbl costs 594720.9475 $. Which unit cost and binding pair does the engine print for it?', '["87.3377 $/bbl, binding Cetane number and Density.", "99.1202 $/bbl, binding Cetane number and Density.", "99.1202 $/bbl, binding Sulfur and Viscosity at 40 C.", "99.1202 $/bbl, binding Cetane number and Flash point."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The AGO result prints Unit cost 99.1202 $/bbl and Binding: Cetane number and Density. 87.3377 $/bbl is the Apapa PMS cargo''s unit cost, and sulfur, viscosity and flash point each carry giveaway on the AGO recipe.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 2, 'A specification declares the mass basis. What are its two weights in the row sum((w_i - L d_i) v_i) <= 0?', '["w_i is the property and d_i is 1, as on the volume basis, with the SG applied afterwards.", "w_i is the property and d_i is SG, so the mass enters the denominator only.", "w_i is SG x the property''s index and d_i is SG, the viscosity weights.", "w_i is SG x the property, and d_i is SG."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis table gives the mass basis w_i = SG x the property and d_i = SG. The index-on-mass basis is the one that puts the property''s index in w_i.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 3, 'At Apapa the template''s Sulfur maximum is 50 ppm, and the components carry 4 ppm (Reformate), 110 ppm (FCC gasoline), 1 ppm (Isomerate) and 1 ppm (Butane). Which component carries a positive coefficient in the sulfur row?', '["FCC gasoline, the one component whose sulfur sits above the 50 ppm limit.", "Reformate, since it carries the most sulfur of the three low-sulfur components.", "All four, because the row counts every barrel''s sulfur toward the limit.", "Isomerate and Butane, the two components carrying 1 ppm each."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On the mass basis the coefficient is SG x the property less 50 x SG, so its sign is the sign of the component''s sulfur against the limit. Only FCC gasoline''s 110 ppm sits above 50.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 4, 'The 9 psi RVP limit enters its row as the index 15.5885. Reformate carries 4.2799, FCC gasoline 9.7834, Isomerate 24.4477 and Butane 142.3287. Which components carry a negative coefficient in the RVP row?', '["Isomerate and Butane, whose indices sit above the 15.5885 limit.", "Butane alone, the component taken up to its availability.", "Reformate and FCC gasoline, whose indices sit below 15.5885.", "None of them, since an index row has no negative weights."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the index basis w_i is the component''s index and d_i is 1, so a coefficient is the component''s index less 15.5885, rvpIndex of the 9 psi limit. Reformate at 4.2799 and FCC gasoline at 9.7834 sit below it.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 5, 'RVP_INDEX_EXPONENT is 1.25. What part does it play in rvpIndex?', '["It is the RVP limit''s index at 9 psi, the figure the row takes.", "It is n in RVPI = RVP^n, the index that RVP blends through.", "It is the slope of the index at the 9 psi limit.", "It is set per template, fixed by the 50 ppm gasoline template."]'::jsonb, 1, 'RVP blends through an index: RVPI = RVP^n, blended on volume and inverted, and RVP_INDEX_EXPONENT is 1.25. The index of the 9 psi limit is 15.5885, and the slope of the index there is 2.1651.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 6, 'rvpIndex takes 12.9 psi to 24.4477, and rvpFromIndex takes 24.4477 back to 12.9000. What does that round trip show?', '["The index is linear in RVP, so it blends as plain volume would.", "The inverse rounds to the nearest tenth of a psi.", "Isomerate''s RVP was clamped to the template limit first.", "The inverse returns the pressure the index started from, 12.9000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every round trip in the table comes back to the pressure it started from: 3.2, 6.2, 9, 12.9 and 52.8 psi. The index is blended, then turned back by the same rule.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 7, 'On the AGO pool, which index does the optimizer''s Viscosity at 40 C row blend through?', '["The Refutas index on mass, the same index the assay studio uses.", "The RVP index at exponent 1.25, applied to viscosity in place of pressure.", "A linear average on volume, the treatment given to cetane and flash point.", "The Refutas index on volume, the ASTM D7152 basis."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Viscosity at 40 C blends through the Refutas index on mass (indexOnMass), the same index as the assay studio, with weights SG x the property''s index and SG. Cetane and flash point are treated linearly on volume. The mass basis is held item C12, stated as a limit.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 8, 'SPEC_TEMPLATES give the 50 ppm gasoline template a Sulfur maximum of 50 ppm and an RVP maximum of 9 psi. What standing do those limits have?', '["They are the regulation in force for PMS, carried into the optimizer so every cargo complies.", "They are fixed, and a user who wants another limit writes a new template in code.", "They are starting points; the regulation in force governs, and every limit is editable.", "They are the buyer''s contract terms, read from the cargo nomination for each run."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The regulation in force governs, and every figure in the course is invented and illustrative. The template is a shape to start from.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 9, 'propertyOfBlend recomputes each Apapa property from the finished recipe, and every difference against optimiseBlend is 0.0000, including Sulfur at 50.0000 and RVP at 9.0000. What does that agreement show?', '["The two functions share the LP rows, so the agreement holds by construction and checks nothing.", "The recipe was solved twice with the same rows, and both solves landed on one vertex.", "Sulfur and RVP were typed as targets, so the recipe is built to hit them to the digit.", "It is a check on the recipe, since a route apart from the LP rows gives the same figure."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two-route table prints a difference of 0.0000 on every row. The second route recomputes each property by its own rule and does not read the LP rows.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 10, 'The Density specification of the 50 ppm gasoline template runs from 0.72 to 0.775 kg/l. How does it enter the kernel?', '["As one equation fixing the density at the midpoint of the range.", "As two rows, a maximum and a minimum.", "As a bound on each component''s volume, taken from its density.", "As one row on the nearer end, chosen after the recipe is solved."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A range gives one row for each end. The Apapa price table lists them separately as the Density maximum and the Density minimum.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 11, 'The AGO template treats cetane and flash point linearly on volume. How does the template itself describe that treatment?', '["As a screening approximation, for both cetane and flash point.", "As the exact rule, which is why neither has an index.", "As a mass basis in effect, since cetane and flash point follow the specific gravity.", "As a held limit, so no recipe may bind on cetane or flash point."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The template notes that treating cetane and flash point linearly on volume is a screening approximation. The AGO recipe binds on Cetane number and Density.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 12, 'The AGO recipe''s viscosity reads 3.0036 cSt with the Refutas index on mass, the engine''s basis, and 2.9518 cSt with the index on volume. Which reading is correct?', '["2.9518 cSt, because ASTM D7152 blends on volume and so it supersedes the engine''s figure.", "3.0036 cSt, since it is the basis the engine names.", "The course holds the basis open, and both are printed.", "Neither of them, since the engine refuses a viscosity where the two bases disagree."]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is held item C12. The engine blends on mass and names it, "Refutas index on mass fraction"; the choice of basis is a course and owner decision the course does not settle.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 13, 'Re-solved with the viscosity index blended on volume instead of mass, the AGO recipe keeps every volume, a total cost of 594720.9475 $ and the binding pair Cetane number and Density. What did the basis change on this pool?', '["The recipe, because every row built on an index moves when its weights change basis.", "The binding list, which gains Viscosity at 40 C once its index blends on volume.", "Nothing the engine reports, since the viscosity figure is kept on mass either way.", "The achieved viscosity alone, since viscosity is not a row the recipe is pressed against."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The achieved viscosity moves from 3.0036 cSt to 2.9518 cSt. With viscosity not binding, the recipe stays where the cetane and density rows hold it.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 14, 'The AGO recipe''s viscosity of 3.0036 cSt sits inside the template range of 2 to 4.5 cSt with a giveaway of 1.0036. What is that giveaway measured against?', '["The 4.5 cSt maximum, since a viscosity specification is a maximum first.", "The nearer end of the range, the 2 cSt minimum.", "The midpoint of the range, the target a range specification aims for.", "The 2.9518 cSt reading on volume, the other basis for the same barrels."]'::jsonb, 1, 'For a range, giveaway is the smaller of the two gaps, so 1.0036 is the distance to the nearer end.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-blending-rules-as-rows', 15, 'The Automotive gasoil, 50 ppm sulfur template carries a Density specification. What range does it set, and on which basis does density blend there?', '["0.82 to 0.845 kg/l, on volume.", "0.72 to 0.775 kg/l, on volume.", "0.82 to 0.845 kg/l, on mass.", "No minimum and 0.991 kg/l, on volume."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The gasoil template carries Density on volume from 0.82 to 0.845 kg/l. 0.72 to 0.775 kg/l is the range of both gasoline templates, and 0.991 kg/l with no minimum is the fuel oil template''s.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 1, 'The Apapa result prints "Components at their availability: Butane." Butane sits at 400.0000 bbl and Reformate at 3284.6899 bbl against 3500 available. What does that line report?', '["The kernel fills Butane first and then stops adding to it.", "Butane has run out, so the recipe is short of its 8000 bbl target by the missing barrels.", "Reformate is at a bound too, the most the rows let it take.", "Butane''s upper bound holds exactly, and no other bound does."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A component at its availability is a bound holding exactly, one of the constraints that pin the vertex. Reformate at 3284.6899 bbl is inside its 3500 bbl, and the total is 8000.0000 bbl.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 2, 'In the Apapa recipe, what share of the 8000 bbl cargo is FCC gasoline, and what does it cost over the batch?', '["A volume fraction of 0.4106, costing 308103.9109 $.", "A volume fraction of 0.4414, costing 299792.2634 $.", "A volume fraction of 0.4414, costing 308103.9109 $.", "A volume fraction of 0.0980, costing 69165.3861 $."]'::jsonb, 1, 'FCC gasoline enters at 3531.1221 bbl, a volume fraction of 0.4414, at a cost of 299792.2634 $. The 0.4106 and 308103.9109 $ row is Reformate, and 0.0980 with 69165.3861 $ is Isomerate.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 3, 'The Apapa pool has four variables. Which four constraints hold exactly at its optimum?', '["The batch row and the RON, MON and density rows, each met at its template limit.", "The four upper bounds, each component at its availability.", "The batch row, Butane''s upper bound, the sulfur row and the RVP row.", "The sulfur and RVP rows, each counted as row and bound."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A vertex needs at least as many constraints and bounds holding exactly as there are variables. At Apapa they are the batch row, Butane at its availability, and the binding Sulfur and RVP rows; RON, MON and Density carry giveaway.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 4, 'The Apapa result lists Sulfur and RVP as binding. What does the course say a binding specification is?', '["Met with a giveaway of 3.5010, so it is the row holding the octane up.", "Met exactly, with the optimum pressed against it, so relaxing it lowers the cost.", "Met anywhere inside its range, so relaxing it leaves the cost where it is.", "Typed by the planner as a must-hit target, so the recipe is built to land on it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A binding specification is met exactly; the optimum is pressed against it, and relaxing it lowers the cost. At Apapa Sulfur achieves 50.0000 against 50 and RVP 9.0000 against 9, each with a giveaway of 0.0000. 3.5010 is RON''s giveaway, and RON does not bind.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 5, 'RON is achieved at 94.5010 against its minimum of 91. What giveaway does the engine report, and by which rule?', '["3.4928, the limit minus the achieved value, the rule the engine keeps for a maximum.", "0.0000, since a minimum that is met gives nothing away.", "94.5010, the achieved value itself, the octane the cargo delivers.", "3.5010, the achieved value minus the limit for a minimum."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Giveaway is how far inside its limit the blend sits. For a minimum that is the achieved value minus the limit. 3.4928 is the MON giveaway.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 6, 'Density is achieved at 0.7547 kg/l inside a range of 0.72 to 0.775, with a giveaway of 0.0203. Which gap is that?', '["The gap to the 0.72 minimum, since a density range is measured from its floor.", "The sum of the gaps to both ends, the room the blend has across the range.", "The gap to the 0.775 maximum, which is the nearer of the two ends.", "The gap to the midpoint, the target of a range."]'::jsonb, 2, 'For a range the engine reports the smaller of the two gaps. The blend sits nearer the maximum, and 0.0203 kg/l is that distance.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 7, 'The AGO cargo at the same terminal binds on Cetane number and Density, and there sulfur gives away 21.8467 ppm. What does that say about binding?', '["A sulfur limit binds on every gasoline template and never on a gasoil template.", "A specification that binds on one pool can give quality away on another.", "The AGO recipe is off specification on sulfur, since a gasoil must meet its limit exactly.", "Sulfur binds only where the template carries it on mass, and the AGO sulfur row is on volume."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At Apapa Sulfur binds with a giveaway of 0.0000. On the AGO pool Sulfur is not binding and gives away 21.8467 ppm, and both templates carry sulfur on mass.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 8, 'A binding specification shows a giveaway of 0.0000 and binding true. What does the Apapa table show about those two columns in every row?', '["A true can carry a positive giveaway when the limit is a range and the recipe sits inside both ends.", "The columns are independent: binding is typed by the user and giveaway is computed by the engine.", "Every false carries a giveaway below one unit of its property, whatever the specification.", "Each zero giveaway is a true."]'::jsonb, 3, 'In the Apapa table the two zero giveaways, Sulfur and RVP, are the two trues, and RON, MON and Density carry giveaway with binding false.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 9, 'Why does the Apapa recipe deliver a RON of 94.5010 against a RON minimum of 91?', '["RON does not bind, and the blend does not touch its 91 minimum.", "The optimizer adds octane on purpose, as a safety margin.", "RON blends on mass, so the volume figure overstates what the buyer receives in the tank.", "RON binds at 94.5010, the value the template sets for the cargo."]'::jsonb, 0, 'RON''s binding is false, with a giveaway of 3.5010 on the volume basis. A non-binding specification has a price of zero: relieving a limit the blend does not touch saves nothing.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 10, 'valueGiveaway prices the MON giveaway of 3.4928 at a typed 0.4 $ per unit per bbl. What does it report over the 8000 bbl batch?', '["16804.7174 $, the figure printed for the RON giveaway.", "11176.9355 $, the MON gap valued at the typed unit value.", "not priced, the entry for a gap with no unit value", "0.0000 $, since MON does not bind and its row is slack"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The MON row reads a giveaway of 3.4928, a typed unit value of 0.4 and 11176.9355 $. The RON row reads 16804.7174 $, and Density reads not priced.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 11, 'The RON giveaway of 3.5010 is valued at 16804.7174 $ over the batch. Where does the worth of one octane number come from?', '["From the kernel, which reads a price per octane number off the RON row''s dual at the optimum.", "From a market feed of octane premiums at run time.", "From the user, who typed a unit value of 0.6 $ per unit per bbl.", "From the 50 ppm gasoline template, which carries a value beside each limit."]'::jsonb, 2, 'valueGiveaway takes a value per unit from the user. The 0.6 is the typed unit value on the RON row, and the RON row''s own value of relief is 0.0000.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 12, 'Density''s giveaway of 0.0203 kg/l has no unit value typed. What does valueGiveaway report for it?', '["0.0000 $, since a blank unit value is read as a price of zero", "a price at the RON unit value of 0.6, carried across to every row", "an error, because every row of the table needs a unit value", "not priced"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Where no unit value is given the gap is reported without a price. A figure of zero would be a price nobody typed.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 13, 'The giveaway value table lists RON and MON and Density, and leaves out Sulfur and RVP. Why?', '["They are binding, so they give nothing away and there is nothing to price.", "Sulfur and RVP blend on mass and index, and valueGiveaway only prices properties blended on volume.", "Their unit values were left blank, so the rows are dropped.", "Their units are ppm and psi, which valueGiveaway cannot price."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The table says so in its own line: specifications with no giveaway (binding) are not listed: Sulfur, RVP.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 14, 'The RON minimum''s value of relief is 0.0000 while the RON giveaway, priced at the typed 0.6, is 16804.7174 $. How do the two figures sit together?', '["One of them is wrong, since a limit with priced giveaway must carry a value of relief.", "They answer different questions: what moving the limit saves, and what the delivered quality is worth.", "The giveaway value is the relief price times the volume blended over the batch.", "The relief price is the giveaway value per barrel, spread over the 8000 bbl."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A non-binding specification has a price of zero, and RON does not bind. valueGiveaway values the RON gap at the user''s typed 0.6 over the volume blended.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-binding-and-giveaway', 15, 'Which total cost and unit cost does the Apapa recipe print?', '["698701.5605 $ and 87.3377 $/bbl.", "698701.5605 $ and 87.5108 $/bbl.", "698569.3341 $ and 87.3377 $/bbl.", "710560.2149 $ and 87.3377 $/bbl."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Apapa recipe totals 8000.0000 bbl at 698701.5605 $, unit cost 87.3377 $/bbl. 87.5108 is the volume row''s price, the marginal barrel; 698569.3341 $ and 710560.2149 $ are the recipes with Butane''s maximum left blank and typed as 0.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 1, 'The textbook case re-solved with row 1 raised to rhs 25 gives an objective of 21.7500, a change of 0.7500, and the kernel''s shadow price on row 1 is 0.7500. What does the agreement show?', '["A shadow price is the objective divided by the right-hand side, so it moves whenever either of them moves on a re-solve.", "A shadow price is the change in the optimum per unit of a row''s right-hand side.", "Row 1 has spare capacity, so its price is set by row 2 alone.", "The shadow price is row 1''s coefficient on x, read off the row as written."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One more unit of row 1''s right-hand side is worth 0.7500 of objective. The re-solve on row 2, rhs 7, gives 21.5000 and a change of 0.5000, the same agreement.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 2, 'At Apapa the Sulfur maximum row prints a rowPrice of -0.0914 and a price of 551.8026 per ppm. Which figure is a price a planner reads?', '["-0.0914, which is the dollars saved per ppm with its sign turned the way the kernel keeps it.", "Both, since they are the same dual printed at two different roundings of the same number.", "The price, 551.8026 $ per ppm of the sulfur maximum.", "Neither, since a binding row is priced only by re-solving the recipe at another limit."]'::jsonb, 2, 'rowPrice is the change in cost per unit of the row''s right-hand side. For a specification row it is not a price per ppm or per psi: moving the limit by one unit moves the row by sum(d_i v_i), and the engine scales the dual into 551.8026 $ per ppm.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 3, 'The sulfur row''s scale is sum(SG x volume) over the recipe, 6037.3872. Why does the dual need multiplying by it?', '["Moving the limit by one ppm moves the row by sum(d_i v_i), and on the mass basis d_i is SG.", "It converts the batch from barrels into tonnes, since the price is quoted per tonne.", "6037.3872 is the batch''s sulfur content, and the dual is quoted per unit of it.", "It turns the sulfur maximum into a minimum, so relief and cost move together."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The limit L sits inside every coefficient w_i - L d_i. rowPrice x 6037.3872 is -551.8026, the change in cost per ppm of the limit.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 4, 'rowPrice x scale on the sulfur row is -551.8026, and the reported value of relief is 551.8026 $ per ppm. Why the opposite signs?', '["A minimum''s relief is dCost/dL, and the sulfur row is a minimum, so the price keeps the sign the dual gives it.", "The scale is negative on a mass row, since sulfur is carried against the specific gravity of the batch.", "Rounding the dual to four places flips the sign of the product.", "Relief on a maximum is the negative of dCost/dL, and raising it lowers cost."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Raising the maximum lowers the cost, so dCost/dL is negative and the saving is positive. A maximum''s relief is the negative of dCost/dL and a minimum''s is dCost/dL.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 5, 'The RVP row gives -2054.8893 $ per index point, from rowPrice -0.2569 times the 8000.0000 bbl batch. Why is that still not the answer to what a psi of relief is worth?', '["The batch must be replaced by sum(SG x volume), because RVP blends on mass through the index at the terminal.", "The figure is per barrel of the batch, and it still needs multiplying by 8000.0000.", "The limit sits in the row as an index, so index points still have to be turned into psi.", "RVP relief carries no value at Apapa, since RVP gives away nothing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the index basis d_i is 1, so the scale is the batch volume, and the result is dollars per index point. The slope of the index at the limit, dIndex/dL, turns index points into psi: 4448.9659 $ per psi.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 6, 'The value of RVP relief, 4448.9659 $ per psi, divided by the negative of the per-index figure gives 2.1651 index points per psi. RVP_INDEX_EXPONENT x 9^(RVP_INDEX_EXPONENT - 1) also gives 2.1651. What does that agreement show?', '["The price carries exactly the index slope at the limit.", "The index is linear in RVP, so its slope is the same at every pressure and the limit could be read anywhere.", "The exponent 1.25 was fitted to the Apapa pool so the two routes would meet at this one cargo.", "The RVP row is scaled by sum(SG x volume), 6037.3872, which the index slope cancels."]'::jsonb, 0, '2.1651 index points per psi is the slope of the index at the 9 psi limit, and the same slope from the exported exponent is 2.1651. The RVP row is in index units and scales by the 8000.0000 bbl batch.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 7, 'Why is the slope of the RVP index read at 9 psi and at no other pressure?', '["It is Butane''s RVP, since Butane is the component at its availability.", "The price uses dIndex/dL, the index''s slope at the limit L, which is 9 psi.", "The exponent is defined at 9 psi, and RVP_INDEX_EXPONENT holds only there.", "It is the RVP of FCC gasoline, the largest component in the recipe."]'::jsonb, 1, 'optimiseBlend reports price = rowPrice x sum(d_i v_i) x dIndex/dL. At Apapa that slope, at the 9 psi limit, is 2.1651 index points per psi.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 8, 'The Total volume row at Apapa reads 87.5108 as its price and 87.5108 as its rowPrice. Why do the two columns agree on this row?', '["The volume row is non-binding, so both of its columns carry the same default figure of the unit cost.", "The engine copies rowPrice into the price column for any row that sits on the volume basis.", "Both columns print the average barrel, which is the cargo''s unit cost.", "Its right-hand side is already barrels of product, so the dual is dollars per barrel."]'::jsonb, 3, 'For the volume row the dual is already the cost of one more barrel of product. A specification row''s dual is not a price per unit of the property: the AGO cetane row blends on volume and prints rowPrice 0.1932 against a price of 1159.3909.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 9, 'At Apapa the marginal barrel is 87.5108 $/bbl, the unit cost is 87.3377 $/bbl, and marginal minus average is 0.1731. What makes the next barrel dearer than the average?', '["The next barrel has to meet a tighter sulfur limit than the first 8000 barrels, since every added barrel dilutes the batch.", "The kernel adds a handling margin to the marginal barrel that the unit cost leaves out.", "Butane sits at its availability and cannot supply the next barrel, so it is made from the other three.", "The unit cost is read on the 50 ppm template and the marginal barrel on the 10 ppm one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The component at its availability cannot supply the next barrel, so the next barrel is made from the others. The average spreads the cost of every barrel already blended, Butane included.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 10, 'The Apapa cargo is re-solved at 8001 bbl, and the cost rises by 87.5108. What does that check?', '["The volume row''s price is a cost the kernel reproduces.", "That the unit cost of 87.3377 $/bbl is what one more barrel adds to the total cost of the cargo.", "That the marginal barrel is the unit cost plus a fixed handling charge the kernel adds per extra barrel.", "That Butane''s availability rises by one barrel whenever the batch rises by one barrel on a re-solve."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Solved again at 8001 bbl, the cost rises by exactly the price the volume row reported. The marginal barrel is a real cost, and the unit cost is a different figure.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 11, 'The optimizer''s default 1000 bbl pool prints marginal barrel minus unit cost of 0.0000 $/bbl, with nothing at its availability. Why do its two barrels cost the same?', '["Isomerate sits at zero, and a component at zero sets the price of the marginal barrel.", "With no availability limit pressing, every row but the volume row has a zero right-hand side, so cost scales with the batch.", "The batch is small, and at 1000 bbl the kernel rounds the gap away to 0.0000.", "No specification binds in the default pool, so every row but volume prices at zero."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At Apapa the gap appears because Butane sits at a limit the next barrel cannot move. In the default pool no bound presses, Sulfur and RVP still bind, and both figures print 86.1228.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 12, 'Raising the Apapa sulfur maximum to 51 ppm prints a saving of 551.6796 $, lowering it to 49 prints a saving of -551.9256 $, and the shadow price is 551.8026 $ per ppm. What explains three different figures?', '["The re-solves were run on a different pool, so their savings measure another recipe entirely.", "The shadow price is read at the 51 ppm re-solve, so it prices a different limit from the recipe''s.", "Four-place rounding of the re-solved totals opens the gap.", "The price is a derivative at the optimum; a whole unit of relief is a finite move."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A shadow price is the value of relief at the margin, a derivative at the optimum; the saving from one whole unit of relief is a different number, and the re-solve table prints both savings beside the price of 551.8026 $ per ppm.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 13, 'The RVP shadow price at Apapa is 4448.9659 $ per psi. Where does the digest read what relieving the maximum by one whole psi saves?', '["From the shadow price, since it holds for any move of the RVP maximum, however large.", "From the average of the two re-solves, taken as the value of a psi.", "From the re-solve at an RVP limit of 10, which prints 3651.3942 $.", "From the unit cost of 87.3377 $/bbl, taken over the batch."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine re-solved with the limit moved one whole unit each way, and relieving the 9 psi maximum to 10 prints a saving of 3651.3942 $ against the price of 4448.9659 $ per psi.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 14, 'The AGO Density maximum is priced at 852453.4687 $ per kg/l. What must a reader hold in mind?', '["It is the value of the whole AGO cargo, which the density row carries because it is binding at the optimum.", "The price is per whole kg/l of the density limit.", "It is a rowPrice, so it has to be multiplied by the 6000.0000 bbl scale before a planner can read it.", "It is the cost of the giveaway on density, which the engine prices without a unit value being typed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A price per unit of the property is per whole unit of it, so the density relief is dollars per kg/l. The row prints rowPrice -142.0756, scale 6000.0000 and rowPrice x scale -852453.4687.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-shadow-prices', 15, 'On the AGO recipe the cetane and density rows scale by 6000.0000, and the sulfur and viscosity rows by 5070.0000. What decides which scale a row takes?', '["Its basis: a volume row scales by the batch volume, and a mass row by sum(SG x volume).", "Whether it is a maximum: maximums take the batch and minimums take the mass sum.", "The sign of its rowPrice: a negative dual takes the batch, a positive one the mass sum.", "Whether it blends through an index: index rows take the mass sum, the rest the batch."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The scale is sum(d_i v_i). On the volume basis d_i is 1, and on the mass basis it is SG, which gives 5070.0000 on this recipe. The cetane minimum takes the batch with a positive rowPrice of 0.1932, and sulfur takes the mass sum without an index.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 1, 'The Apapa pool is asked for the 10 ppm gasoline template and the engine returns the status infeasible. What recipe comes back with it?', '["The closest recipe it could find, with the limits it breaks marked so the planner can see them.", "The 50 ppm recipe, returned unchanged as a fallback cargo for the same 8000 bbl batch.", "None; the whole answer is the verdict and its message.", "A recipe with the sulfur maximum relaxed back to 50."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine returns the status infeasible and "REFUSED: No recipe from these components can meet every specification. Relax a limit, or bring in a component that can." Moving the sulfur maximum back to 50 alone still reads infeasible.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 2, 'Each limit of the 10 ppm template is moved back to its 50 ppm value in turn, the rest kept at 10 ppm. What does the table show?', '["All four rows stay infeasible: no single limit moved back rescues the pool.", "Moving the RON minimum back to 91 rescues the pool on its own.", "Moving the sulfur maximum back to 50 makes the pool optimal.", "Moving the RVP maximum back to 9 rescues it, since RVP binds at the Apapa optimum."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The RON, MON, sulfur and RVP rows each still read infeasible. No single limit moved back rescues the pool.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 3, 'On the 50 ppm template, one limit at a time is tightened to its 10 ppm value. Which single tightenings still leave the Apapa pool a recipe?', '["The RON minimum of 95 and the sulfur maximum of 10, because neither of those two rows binds at the 50 ppm optimum today.", "None of the four: each single tightening reads infeasible.", "All four: each single tightening still returns optimal.", "The MON minimum of 85 and the RVP maximum of 8.5."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Tightened one at a time, the RON minimum and the sulfur maximum are each infeasible, and MON and RVP return optimal.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 4, 'The Apapa pool is posed with a RON minimum of 99. What does the engine return?', '["An optimal recipe with RON at 94.5010, since RON gives away 3.5010 at the Apapa optimum.", "The status infeasible, with the message that no recipe from these components can meet every specification.", "An optimal recipe with the RON specification listed as skipped, with its reason.", "The refusal for a missing cost, since no component''s cost can buy a RON of 99."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A RON minimum of 99 sits in the digest''s infeasible table beside the 10 ppm template, with the same message. An infeasible blend is a real answer: the specifications cannot be met by the components available.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 5, 'Butane''s maximum is typed as 0 because the tank is empty. What does the engine return?', '["Optimal with Butane at 438.1863 bbl, since a zero maximum is read as no limit and the specifications decide.", "A refusal, because a maximum of zero leaves the component with no room and must be removed from the pool by hand.", "Optimal, with Butane at 0.0000 bbl, a cost of 710560.2149 $, and Sulfur alone binding.", "The Apapa recipe unchanged, Butane at 400.0000 bbl and 698701.5605 $."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A typed 0 is none. The recipe comes back with Butane at 0.0000 bbl, and the binding list is Sulfur alone.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 6, 'Butane''s maximum is left blank, and the recipe takes 438.1863 bbl of it at 698569.3341 $. How has the engine read the blank?', '["As the 400 bbl tank, the default the engine keeps for a blank field.", "As a maximum of 0, the reading the engine gives a typed zero.", "As a default cap the engine sets on any component left blank.", "As no limit: a maximum left blank sets no bound on Butane''s volume."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A maximum left blank is no limit. The engine returns optimal with Butane at 438.1863 bbl and binding Sulfur, RVP; a typed 0 is none, and gives Butane at 0.0000 bbl.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 7, 'optimiseBlend is called with no components at all. What does it return?', '["REFUSED: No components to blend.", "The status infeasible, with no recipe.", "Optimal, with an empty recipe.", "The kernel throws on a malformed row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The refusals table prints the request with no components beside "REFUSED: No components to blend."'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 8, 'The Apapa pool is solved with Isomerate''s cost field empty. What comes back from the optimizer?', '["An optimal recipe with Isomerate at a cost of zero, taking as much as the specifications and its tank allow, because a blank cost is read as a free component and the least-cost recipe leans on it as far as the rows let it go.", "An optimal recipe with Isomerate left out entirely, since a component without a cost is dropped from the pool silently and the other three components are blended to the same 8000 bbl target instead.", "REFUSED: No cost for Isomerate. A least-cost recipe needs a price on every component; remove the component or give it one.", "An infeasible status, since phase one cannot build an objective row for a component whose cost is missing, and so the pool is reported as unable to meet every specification at the 8000 bbl batch."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A blank cost is refused. The cost column is the objective, and a least-cost recipe needs a price on every component.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 9, 'A blank maximum is solved and a blank cost is refused. What makes the two blanks different?', '["A blank maximum is read as zero, while a blank cost is read as a free component.", "A blank maximum is no limit; a least-cost recipe needs a price on each component.", "The kernel checks costs itself and throws, while the optimizer checks maximums.", "A maximum can never be typed, so every maximum is blank and the engine sets it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A maximum left blank is no limit: Butane left blank solves at 438.1863 bbl. A blank cost is refused, with Isomerate named.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 10, 'A target volume of 0 returns "REFUSED: The target volume must be greater than zero." Which reading of that answer holds?', '["It is the optimizer''s word for unbounded, used when the batch row has no upper limit.", "It is an optimal empty recipe, since a batch of zero costs nothing to blend.", "It is the kernel throwing on a malformed row with the wrong coefficient count.", "It is one of the inputs optimiseBlend refuses, each in its own words."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It sits among what optimiseBlend refuses, each in its own words. The infeasible pools also print a REFUSED message, and they carry the status infeasible beside it.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 11, 'Isomerate carries no sulfur figure. The engine returns optimal at 695245.0644 $ with Sulfur skipped. What does that total cost?', '["A recipe meeting every specification except the skipped sulfur row.", "The Apapa cargo on the full 50 ppm template, since the missing figure is read as zero sulfur on Isomerate and the rest is unchanged.", "The full Apapa cargo on the 50 ppm template.", "Sulfur''s price of relief, read over the batch."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The reason reads "Not every component carries this property, so the specification was not applied." The Apapa cargo with sulfur applied costs 698701.5605 $.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 12, 'FCC gasoline has no SG and no API, yet every component still carries a sulfur figure. Why is the Sulfur specification skipped?', '["FCC gasoline''s sulfur of 110 ppm is above the limit, and a component above the limit cannot be carried in a sulfur row at all.", "Sulfur blends on mass, and a mass row needs every density.", "The engine skips any specification that would bind at the optimum when a component''s data is incomplete in any column.", "Without an SG the engine cannot form the batch row, so every specification on that component is skipped with it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the mass basis w_i is SG x sulfur and d_i is SG. The engine says so: "This property blends on mass and not every component has a density (sg or API), so the specification was not applied."'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 13, 'With Sulfur skipped because Isomerate carries no sulfur figure, what does the recipe report as achieved for Sulfur, and for Density?', '["Sulfur at 0.0000 ppm, the missing figure read as none, and Density at 0.7536 kg/l.", "Sulfur at 50.0000 and Density at 0.7547, as in the full Apapa recipe.", "Neither is reported, since a skipped row blanks every achieved value.", "Sulfur is not formed and returns no value; Density, which blends on volume, is applied at 0.7536 kg/l."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The skipped specification is not formed: the engine returns no value. Density blends on volume, is applied, and achieves 0.7536 kg/l on that recipe.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 14, 'Reformate is typed with a minimum of 3000 and a maximum of 2000. The kernel alone would call such a problem infeasible. What does the optimizer return?', '["The status infeasible from phase one, with the specifications listed so the planner can hunt for the conflicting pair.", "A recipe with Reformate at 2000 bbl, its maximum.", "REFUSED: Reformate has a minimum above its maximum.", "A recipe with Reformate at 3000 bbl, its minimum."]'::jsonb, 2, 'optimiseBlend refuses the crossed bounds and names the component. The kernel alone returns infeasible for a lower bound of 5 above an upper bound of 3.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-infeasible-refused-and-skipped', 15, 'Isomerate is given a floor of 1200 bbl. The recipe takes 1200.0000 bbl at 698909.5490 $, Butane falls to 339.9340 bbl, and sulfur relief is 537.5403 $ per ppm. Why does that relief differ from the 551.8026 at the Apapa optimum?', '["The floor adds its own cost to every row, and the relief price subtracts it once per barrel of the floor.", "The floor forces a different recipe, with Butane inside its availability.", "Sulfur no longer binds once Isomerate is forced in, so its row is priced at the floor instead of the limit.", "Butane rises to its availability of 400, which lowers the sulfur price."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A minimum forces barrels into the recipe. At that optimum butane is 339.9340 bbl against its availability of 400: inside its availability. Sulfur and RVP still bind.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 1, 'In the optimizer''s default pool, what is relief worth on its two binding specifications?', '["Sulfur 551.8026 $ per ppm and RVP 4448.9659 $ per psi.", "Sulfur 55.0114 $ per ppm and RVP 4448.9659 $ per psi.", "Sulfur -0.0720 $ per ppm and RVP -0.2674 $ per psi.", "Sulfur 55.0114 $ per ppm and RVP 578.9052 $ per psi."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The default pool''s price table prints 55.0114 $ per ppm on the Sulfur maximum and 578.9052 $ per psi on the RVP maximum, with the rowPrices -0.0720 and -0.2674 beside them. 551.8026 and 4448.9659 are the Apapa cargo''s figures.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 2, 'When the productBlending oracle checks a recipe, where do the achieved properties it compares come from?', '["From physical inventories, with its rows from physical mass balances.", "From propertyOfBlend, called on the engine''s own recipe.", "From the engine''s LP rows, read back at the optimum.", "From the Refutas index inverted by bisection, for all of them."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The oracle_productblending.py row reads: rows built from physical mass balances, solved by exact rational vertex enumeration; properties from physical inventories.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 3, 'The optimizer''s default pool blends Reformate 516.0028, FCC gasoline 414.8506, Isomerate 0.0000 and Butane 69.1466 bbl. What does it report about its bounds?', '["Butane at its availability, as in the Apapa cargo.", "Components at their availability: nothing. At zero: Isomerate.", "Every component at a bound, either its availability or zero.", "Isomerate is skipped, since it takes no barrels."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The default pool prints "Components at their availability: nothing. At zero: Isomerate." With no availability limit pressing, its marginal barrel and unit cost both print 86.1228.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 4, 'What does the Product Blending Optimizer show with nothing typed?', '["The Apapa PMS pool, 8000 bbl, on the 50 ppm template.", "Its default gasoline pool, 1000 bbl, on the 10 ppm template.", "Its default gasoline pool, 1000 bbl, on the 50 ppm template.", "The Apapa AGO pool, 6000 bbl, on the 50 ppm diesel template."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With nothing typed the optimizer shows its default gasoline pool, 1000 bbl, the 50 ppm template. The Apapa PMS cargo is 8000 bbl and the AGO cargo 6000 bbl.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 5, 'The default pool''s price table prints rowPrice -0.0720 on the Sulfur maximum and -0.2674 on the RVP maximum. How are those figures read?', '["As the rows'' duals, which are not prices per ppm or per psi.", "As the dollars saved per ppm and per psi, turned negative because the default pool is a maximisation of margin.", "As the binding tolerances the kernel allows on each row.", "As the giveaway on the sulfur and RVP rows, in ppm and psi."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The prices of relief are 55.0114 $ per ppm and 578.9052 $ per psi. rowPrice is kept beside each.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 6, 'A planner poses an LP whose coefficients run into millions, far from the barrel-scale cargoes of the two apps. Given held item L4, how should the result be quoted?', '["As wrong, since the course shows that the kernel fails on any problem scaled in millions and so the recipe cannot be used.", "With the limit beside it: outside the kernel''s shown range.", "As any other result, because the kernel''s tolerances rescale with the largest coefficient in each row of the problem.", "As provisional until the kernel is re-run with its tolerances tightened by the planner to the scale of the problem."]'::jsonb, 1, 'L4: the LP kernel uses absolute tolerances on its pivots and on phase one. They are right for the barrel-scale problems the two apps pose; a problem scaled in millions is outside what it is shown to handle.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 7, 'Held item C12 prints the Obigbo export blend at 7.4743 cSt with the Refutas index on mass and 7.3107 cSt on volume. What does the course do with the two figures?', '["Takes 7.3107 cSt as correct, since ASTM D7152 blends on volume and so the engine''s mass figure is only a screen.", "Takes 7.4743 cSt as correct, since the engine''s basis is the one every other property in the course is blended on.", "Averages the two figures and quotes the mean as the blend.", "States what the engine does and the limit, and leaves the basis to a course and owner decision."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine blends on mass fraction and names it, "Refutas index on mass fraction". The two disagree, both are printed, and no basis is keyed as right.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 8, 'Of the three held items, which one concerns the LP kernel, and what does the engine do there?', '["L4: absolute tolerances on its pivots and on phase one.", "C12: absolute tolerances on the pivots.", "C13: a binding test of 1e-7 times the limit on each row.", "L4: the Refutas index taken on mass fraction in the AGO rows."]'::jsonb, 0, 'SECTION 27 lists three held items: L4, the LP kernel''s absolute tolerances on its pivots and on phase one; C12, the Refutas index on mass fraction; C13, Watson K taken at the blend''s T50.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 9, 'oracle_lp.py checks the kernel on 181 problems (124 optimal, 44 infeasible, 13 unbounded) by exact rational vertex enumeration with no simplex at all. What fact from the first module makes that route possible?', '["The kernel''s absolute tolerances make every problem exact once its coefficients are written as rational numbers.", "Bland''s rule, which picks the entering variable and cannot cycle.", "The optimum of a linear programme, when there is one, sits at a vertex.", "Phase one, which drives the artificial variables to zero."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Enumerating vertices finds the optimum because the optimum, when there is one, is found at a vertex. The oracle does it in exact rationals with no simplex at all.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 10, 'Why do 44 infeasible and 13 unbounded problems sit among the LP oracle''s golden cases?', '["They are problems the kernel cannot solve, kept to show where the tolerances of L4 fail on real cargoes.", "Infeasible and unbounded are answers the kernel returns, like optimal.", "They are malformed problems, kept to check that the kernel throws on a row with the wrong coefficient count.", "They are the cases that the oracle cannot enumerate and so hands back to the simplex kernel for its answer."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The oracle_lp.py row counts 124 optimal, 44 infeasible and 13 unbounded. Infeasible and unbounded are answers; a malformed problem is not, and there the kernel throws.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 11, 'oracle_productblending.py holds the blending engine on 9 pools. How does it compute a price of relief?', '["By reading rowPrice from the engine and multiplying it by the scale the engine printed.", "By the same scaled derivative the engine uses, restated in Python.", "It does not price relief; it checks recipes and properties only.", "By exact re-solve with the limit moved."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The oracle_productblending.py row reads: relief by exact re-solve with the limit moved. Each oracle is written from the rules and not from the JavaScript.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 12, 'The engines state RVP_INDEX_EXPONENT 1.25 and BINDING_TOLERANCE 1e-7 in their modules. What does that give a reader?', '["A value read from the module itself, as the engines state it.", "A regulation, since each constant is the limit the regulation in force sets.", "A graded value the capstone checks to four decimals.", "A default price per unit of relief for every binding row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest lists the constants the engines state, read from the modules: CII_BANDS.STABLE 0.7, CII_BANDS.UNSTABLE 0.9, RVP_INDEX_EXPONENT 1.25 and BINDING_TOLERANCE 1e-7. SPEC_TEMPLATES are starting points; the regulation in force governs.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 13, 'oracle_lp.py holds the kernel to 181 problems. How does it compute shadow prices?', '["By rescaling the kernel''s duals.", "By a whole-unit re-solve, averaged.", "As exact one-sided derivatives, by re-solve.", "It checks statuses and objectives only."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The oracle_lp.py row reads: exact rational vertex enumeration with no simplex at all; shadow prices as exact one-sided derivatives by re-solve.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 14, 'Reading the Apapa pool and the default pool side by side, which reading holds in both?', '["The marginal and average barrels print the same figure.", "The same two specifications bind, Sulfur and RVP.", "Butane sits at its availability in both pools.", "Isomerate enters the recipe at zero in both pools."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both pools bind on Sulfur and RVP. The marginal barrel differs from the average at Apapa by 0.1731 and matches it in the default pool, where nothing sits at its availability.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 15, 'The digest counts the refusals it prints: 17. What stands behind that count?', '["Each was asserted against the engine before it was printed.", "Each was written by the course from the engine''s rules, so the wording is the course''s own paraphrase.", "Each is a status the kernel returns from phase one.", "Each is graded against the capstone''s own figures."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest closes: "Refusals printed in this digest, each asserted against the engine before it was printed: 17."'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'In the optimizer''s default pool Isomerate enters at 0.0000 bbl. In the kernel''s terms, what holds exactly for Isomerate there?', '["Its availability, the upper bound, which the result reports as a tank drawn full.", "Its floor of 1200 bbl, the minimum the Isomerate case sets.", "Its lower bound of zero on the Isomerate volume.", "No constraint at all, because a component at zero is dropped from the problem."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The default pool reports "At zero: Isomerate" and "Components at their availability: nothing". A volume on a bound of zero is a bound holding exactly, one of the constraints that can pin a vertex.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'At Apapa the sulfur dual is scaled by sum(SG x volume), 6037.3872, while the RVP dual is scaled by the 8000.0000 bbl batch. What decides the difference?', '["The basis each row declares: d_i is SG on the mass basis and 1 on the index-on-volume basis.", "Whether the row binds: a binding row is scaled by the batch and a slack row by its mass.", "The sense of the row: a maximum is scaled by mass and a minimum by the batch.", "The template: the 50 ppm gasoline template scales every row by mass except its RVP row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The scale is sum(d_i v_i), fixed by the declared basis. RVP also needs the index slope at the limit, 2.1651 index points per psi, because its limit sits in the row as an index.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'Three Apapa requests: a target volume of 0, Reformate with a minimum of 3000 and a maximum of 2000, and the 10 ppm gasoline template. Which one does the digest print with the status infeasible?', '["The target volume of 0, which comes back infeasible.", "The crossed Reformate bounds, which come back infeasible.", "All three of them, because each one comes back without a recipe for the cargo.", "The 10 ppm gasoline template asked of the Apapa PMS pool."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest prints the 10 ppm template with the status infeasible and "REFUSED: No recipe from these components can meet every specification. Relax a limit, or bring in a component that can." The other two are among what optimiseBlend refuses, each in its own words: "REFUSED: The target volume must be greater than zero." and "REFUSED: Reformate has a minimum above its maximum."'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'The MON giveaway of 3.4928, priced at a typed 0.4, is worth 11176.9355 $ over the batch. What would lowering the MON minimum below 81 save on the Apapa cargo?', '["11176.9355 $, the value of the giveaway the lower limit would recover from the buyer.", "Nothing: the MON minimum''s value of relief is 0.0000.", "3.4928 $ per barrel, the MON giveaway read as a saving on each barrel of the cargo.", "551.8026 $ per unit, the same as sulfur relief, as every limit on the cargo is priced alike."]'::jsonb, 1, 'MON does not bind, so relieving it moves nothing. The priced giveaway answers what quality already delivered would fetch; the value of relief answers what moving the limit would save.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'With Butane''s maximum typed as 0 the recipe costs 710560.2149 $ and only Sulfur binds. By the engine''s rule, what is the RVP maximum worth in relief in that recipe?', '["4448.9659 $ per psi, the Apapa figure, which belongs to the specification in any pool.", "More than 4448.9659 $ per psi, now that Butane is gone.", "Zero: relieving a limit the blend does not touch saves nothing.", "No figure: a recipe with a tank typed as 0 prints no prices."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A non-binding specification has a price of zero. With Butane''s maximum typed as 0 the binding list is Sulfur alone, so the RVP maximum is not binding and relieving it saves nothing.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'RVP_INDEX_EXPONENT is 1.25. Where does it enter the Apapa problem?', '["In the RVP row''s coefficients and the 9 psi limit''s index, and so in the slope at the limit.", "In the objective, as a cost per psi added to each component''s price per barrel.", "In the bounds of the high-RVP components, which it tightens as the exponent rises.", "Only in the report, where the achieved RVP is turned back into psi after the solve."]'::jsonb, 0, 'RVPI = RVP^n sets both w_i and the limit''s index in the row, and the slope at the limit is RVP_INDEX_EXPONENT x 9^(RVP_INDEX_EXPONENT - 1), which prints 2.1651.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'With Butane at its 400 bbl availability the Apapa cargo costs 698701.5605 $. With the maximum left blank it takes 438.1863 bbl and costs 698569.3341 $. What does the pair show about Butane''s bound at Apapa?', '["The bound was slack: both recipes bind on Sulfur and RVP whatever Butane''s maximum.", "A blank maximum is read as a tank of zero, so the second recipe is a different pool.", "A blank maximum is rounded to a past availability.", "The bound held exactly: with no limit, the recipe takes more Butane."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At Apapa the result prints "Components at their availability: Butane." A maximum left blank is no limit, and that recipe takes 438.1863 bbl of Butane at 698569.3341 $, against 400.0000 bbl at 698701.5605 $ with the bound in place.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'The Apapa cargo is re-solved with the sulfur maximum tightened to 49 ppm. What does it print?', '["698149.8809 $, a saving of 551.6796 $.", "699253.4861 $, a saving of -551.9256 $.", "695050.1663 $, a saving of 3651.3942 $.", "710560.2149 $, with Sulfur alone binding."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The re-solve table prints Sulfur limit 49 at 699253.4861 $, a saving against the optimum of -551.9256 $. 698149.8809 $ is the sulfur limit of 51, 695050.1663 $ the RVP limit of 10, and 710560.2149 $ the recipe with Butane typed as 0.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'The RON minimum of 91 blends on volume. In the ratio row sum((w_i - L d_i) v_i), what are w_i and d_i for RON?', '["w_i is SG x each component''s RON and d_i is SG, as on the mass basis.", "w_i is each component''s RON index and d_i is 1, as on an index on volume.", "w_i is each component''s RON and d_i is 1, the weights of the volume basis.", "w_i is SG x each RON index and d_i is SG, as on an index on mass."]'::jsonb, 2, 'SPEC_TEMPLATES declares RON on volume, and on the volume basis w_i is the property and d_i is 1. The mass basis and the two index bases carry the other weights in the same table.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'The AGO recipe''s density achieves 0.8450 against the template''s 0.82 to 0.845 kg/l. What does the engine report for it?', '["Giveaway 0.0000, binding true, pressed against the 0.845 maximum.", "Giveaway 0.0000, binding false, as 0.8450 sits inside the range.", "Binding true at the 0.82 minimum, the end a range is read from.", "Skipped, since density blends on volume and the AGO rows are on mass."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The AGO table prints Density achieved 0.8450, giveaway 0.0000, binding true, and the price rows list it as the Density maximum at 852453.4687 $ per kg/l.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'At Apapa both the Density maximum row and the Density minimum row price at 0.0000 per kg/l. Why?', '["The two rows cancel, as a range is priced as its maximum''s dual less its minimum''s.", "Density is on the volume basis, and the engine prices only the mass and index rows.", "No unit value was typed for density, so neither is priced.", "The blend''s 0.7547 kg/l sits inside both ends of the range."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Density carries a giveaway of 0.0203 and binds at neither end. A non-binding specification has a price of zero.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'Apapa''s marginal barrel sits 0.1731 $/bbl above its average and the default pool''s sits 0.0000 above. Which difference between the two pools accounts for that?', '["Apapa binds on Sulfur and RVP, and the default pool binds on no specification at all.", "Apapa holds Butane at its availability, and the default pool holds no component at its availability.", "Apapa is solved on the 10 ppm template, and the default pool on the 50 ppm template.", "Apapa blends 8000 bbl, and the default pool''s 1000 bbl is too small for a gap to print."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both pools are on the 50 ppm template and both bind on Sulfur and RVP. What the digest prints differently is availability: Butane at 400.0000 bbl at Apapa, and nothing in the default pool.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'What does the Apapa PMS recipe take of Isomerate?', '["784.1881 bbl, a volume fraction of 0.0980.", "784.1881 bbl, a volume fraction of 0.0500.", "1200.0000 bbl, the volume on its floor.", "1500 bbl, its whole availability."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The recipe prints Isomerate at 784.1881 bbl, a volume fraction of 0.0980, costing 69165.3861 $. 0.0500 is Butane''s fraction, 1200.0000 bbl is the Isomerate floor case, and 1500 bbl is Isomerate''s availability.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'A user types -50 in Butane''s maximum, meaning no limit. What does the engine return?', '["An optimal recipe with Butane unlimited: a negative maximum is read as the blank the user meant, and it takes 438.1863 bbl.", "An optimal recipe with Butane at 0.0000 bbl: a negative maximum is clamped to zero.", "The status infeasible: no volume of zero or more can sit below a maximum of -50 in the kernel''s bounds.", "A refusal that names Butane''s maximum, asks for a number of zero or more, and says to leave it blank for no limit."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns "REFUSED: Butane maximum must be a number of zero or more. Leave a maximum blank for no limit." A maximum left blank is no limit.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'The Apapa pool on the 10 ppm template comes back infeasible. By the method''s own account, what does that verdict mean?', '["The kernel stops after a fixed number of iterations and reports infeasible on timeout.", "The engine tries each limit of the template in turn and gives up after the fourth.", "Phase one could not drive its artificial variables to zero, so no recipe meets every row.", "Phase two visited every vertex and found none as cheap as the 50 ppm recipe."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Phase one finds any point that meets every row by driving artificial variables to zero; if it cannot, the rows contradict and the answer is infeasible. The kernel''s status is always one of optimal, infeasible or unbounded.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'On the AGO recipe, what sulfur does the engine report as achieved, and on which basis does it blend?', '["28.1533 ppm, on volume.", "28.1533 ppm, on mass.", "50.0000 ppm, on mass.", "21.8467 ppm, on mass."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The AGO table prints Sulfur achieved 28.1533 with giveaway 21.8467, binding false, and the diesel template carries Sulfur on mass. 50.0000 is the Apapa PMS sulfur, where the row binds.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'How much Kerosene does the AGO recipe take, and at what cost?', '["1505.9222 bbl, costing 53915.9391 $.", "624.0271 bbl, costing 161434.8562 $.", "2670.0508 bbl, costing 271010.1523 $.", "1505.9222 bbl, costing 161434.8562 $."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The AGO recipe prints Kerosene at 1505.9222 bbl and 161434.8562 $. 624.0271 bbl and 53915.9391 $ are Hydrotreated LCO''s, and 2670.0508 bbl with 271010.1523 $ is Straight-run gasoil.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'In the textbook plant a manager can buy one extra hour on either unit. Reading the kernel''s duals, which purchase raises earnings more?', '["An hour on the first unit, whose row prices at 0.7500 against 0.5000 on the second.", "An hour on the second unit, whose row 2 has the smaller right-hand side of 6.", "Neither: both rows hold exactly, so an extra hour on either is worth nothing.", "Either of them, as the optimum of 21.0000 is shared between the two rows equally."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both rows hold exactly at x 3.0000, y 1.5000, so both carry a dual. Re-solved with row 1 raised to 25 the objective is 21.7500, a change of 0.7500; with row 2 raised to 7 it is 21.5000, a change of 0.5000.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'The AGO recipe achieves a flash point of 73.7403 C against a minimum of 55, with a giveaway of 18.7403. What do the template and the giveaway say about it?', '["It blends through the Refutas index on mass, and 18.7403 is the smaller gap of a range.", "It binds, as a flash point minimum is a safety limit the engine holds exactly.", "It blends linearly on volume, and 18.7403 is the achieved value minus the minimum.", "It is skipped for lack of a density, and 18.7403 is the gap it leaves."]'::jsonb, 2, 'For a minimum, giveaway is the achieved value minus the limit, and the AGO table prints 18.7403. SPEC_TEMPLATES carries Flash point on volume in the gasoil template.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'On the AGO recipe the Cetane number minimum has rowPrice 0.1932 and price 1159.3909 $ per unit, both positive, while the Density maximum''s rowPrice x scale and its price carry opposite signs. Why the difference?', '["Cetane blends on volume and density on mass, so only density''s scale turns the sign.", "A minimum''s relief is dCost/dL, and a maximum''s is its negative.", "The engine flips the sign on any binding row it prices above a thousand dollars.", "Density does not bind, so its signs need not agree."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cetane is a minimum: lowering it is the relief, and its dual already reads as a saving. Density is a maximum, and for a maximum the digest''s rule turns the sign between the dual and the value of relief.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'On the AGO recipe sulfur gives away 21.8467 ppm. What is sulfur relief worth there, and why?', '["551.8026 $ per ppm, the Apapa figure, because the two templates share the 50 ppm limit.", "21.8467 $ per ppm, the giveaway read as the value of relief on the same specification.", "No figure, because the AGO pool prints a price only for its rows on the volume basis.", "0.0000 per ppm, because sulfur does not bind."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The AGO binding list is Cetane number and Density. A specification with giveaway has room to spare, and relieving it saves nothing.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'The Apapa 10 ppm refusal ends with advice. Which two ways out does it name?', '["Relax a limit, or bring in a component that can.", "Relax a limit, or lower the batch until the components on hand can meet it.", "Buy more Butane, or drop Isomerate from the pool and solve the recipe again.", "Re-solve on the 50 ppm template, or make a smaller cargo from the same tanks."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both ways out sit in the last sentence of the engine''s message. The optimizer chooses neither; the status beside the message is infeasible and no recipe comes back.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'With an Isomerate floor of 1200 bbl, Butane falls to 339.9340 bbl. Would the result''s line "Components at their availability" name Butane?', '["Yes: Butane was at its availability at Apapa, and a floor elsewhere leaves that bound alone.", "Yes: a floor on one component forces every other component onto one of its bounds.", "No: 339.9340 bbl is inside its 400 bbl availability.", "Yes: the line names both Butane and Isomerate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At that optimum butane is 339.9340 bbl against its availability of 400: inside its availability. Isomerate sits on its 1200 bbl floor, a lower bound; an availability is an upper bound.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'At the textbook corner x 0.0000, y 3.0000, what does the kernel print?', '["objective 20.0000, status optimal", "objective 12.0000, status optimal", "objective 12.0000, status infeasible", "objective 21.0000, status optimal"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The corner table prints x 0.0000, y 3.0000 at 12.0000 with the status optimal. 20.0000 is the corner x 4.0000, y 0.0000, and 21.0000 the optimum at x 3.0000, y 1.5000.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'In SPEC_TEMPLATES, how does the Fuel oil, 380 cSt template declare Viscosity at 50 C?', '["volume, with a maximum of 380 cSt and no minimum, the same basis as its density", "mass, with a minimum of 380 cSt and no maximum, the same basis as its sulfur", "index, on volume, with a maximum of 380 cSt, the same basis as the RVP rows", "index, on mass, with a maximum of 380 cSt and no minimum"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Viscosity blends through the Refutas index on mass. The fuel oil template also carries Sulfur on mass at 35000 ppm, Density on volume at 0.991 kg/l and Flash point on volume at 60 C.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'oracle_crudeassay.py holds the assay engine to 6 blends, 4 curve cases and 1 blended default curve. How does it find T50?', '["By bisection, the same way it inverts the Refutas index.", "At the first grid point past 50 percent, read straight off the blended curve.", "As the average of the component crudes'' own midpoints, weighted on volume.", "By reading T50 back out of the golden files it checks."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The oracle_crudeassay.py row lists T50 by bisection and Refutas inverted by bisection. It is written from the rules and not from the JavaScript.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Given held item C12, which report of the AGO viscosity matches what the digest prints?', '["2.9518 cSt alone, the volume figure, as the course keys volume as the correct basis.", "The engine''s 3.0036 cSt, stated as the Refutas index blended on mass fraction.", "The mean of 3.0036 and 2.9518 cSt, the engine''s reading between the two bases.", "No viscosity at all, as the engine withholds a figure on a held basis."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine prints 3.0036 cSt with the index on mass and 2.9518 cSt with the same index on volume. C12 is taught as a stated limit and never graded, and the basis is a course and owner decision.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'A buyer asks Apapa for more cargo, and the desk quotes the extra barrels at the unit cost of 87.3377 $/bbl. What does that quote give away?', '["Nothing: every barrel of a least-cost cargo costs the unit cost at the margin, whatever the bounds.", "87.5108 $/bbl on every added barrel, the whole of the marginal barrel''s cost.", "The value of the Butane already blended in the cargo.", "0.1731 $/bbl on the next barrel, the marginal minus the average."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Marginal minus average is 0.1731: the volume row''s price, 87.5108 $/bbl, is the marginal barrel, and the unit cost, 87.3377 $/bbl, is the average barrel.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'The digest solves each textbook corner with both coordinates fixed by bounds, and all four print the status optimal. Why optimal at every one?', '["Each corner is the optimum of the full problem, so all four report one objective.", "Optimal is reported whenever phase two runs, met rows or not.", "Each fixed point is feasible, so the kernel has a point to return.", "The two rows are dropped once both coordinates are fixed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fixing both coordinates leaves one point, and the kernel''s status says whether it meets every row. The objectives are 0.0000, 20.0000, 12.0000 and 21.0000, and 21.0000 is the optimum of the full problem.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'The engine reports Sulfur achieved at 50.0000 with binding true. Who decides that it binds, and how?', '["The engine, by a test against BINDING_TOLERANCE, 1e-7, times the limit (or 1, if larger).", "The user, who ticks binding on the specification before the solve is run.", "The reader, by judging whether 50.0000 is close enough to the limit of 50.", "The template, which marks Sulfur and RVP binding in the 50 ppm gasoline table."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A specification counts as binding when the achieved value is within BINDING_TOLERANCE (1e-7) times the limit (or 1, if larger) of it. The same test marks RVP at 9.0000 binding.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'The AGO viscosity row blends through the Refutas index on mass. What would its price of relief carry, and what is it at the AGO optimum?', '["The 6000.0000 bbl batch alone, as on the cetane row; 1159.3909 $ per cSt.", "sum(SG x volume), 5070.0000, and the index slope; 0.0000 per cSt, as it does not bind.", "The index slope alone, with no scale; 1.0036 per cSt, the figure printed on its row.", "No scale at all, as viscosity rows carry no dual; the price is left unformed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule is price = rowPrice x sum(d_i v_i) x dIndex/dL. On the index-on-mass basis d_i is SG, and the mass rows scale by sum(SG x volume), 5070.0000. Viscosity at 40 C carries a giveaway of 1.0036 and binds at neither end.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'Isomerate is given a floor of 1200 bbl at Apapa. What does the kernel do with that floor as it solves?', '["It writes the floor as an extra row, Isomerate at least 1200 bbl, beside the specifications.", "It fixes Isomerate at 1200 bbl with both bounds and drops it.", "It shifts the floor to the origin, so every specification row sits off zero.", "It refuses the floor, as a minimum above zero needs a matching maximum."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A minimum forces barrels into the recipe; every specification row then sits off zero after the kernel shifts the floor to the origin. The recipe comes back optimal with Isomerate at 1200.0000 bbl.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'The default pool''s RVP relief is 578.9052 $ per psi, with rowPrice -0.2674. Which of the two does a planner quote as the value of one psi?', '["-0.2674, read as the change in cost per psi of the RVP limit at the optimum.", "The product of the two, the price of relief multiplied once more by the rowPrice.", "Either one: the default pool''s 1000 bbl batch makes the two figures equivalent.", "578.9052 $ per psi, the figure in the price column."]'::jsonb, 3, 'rowPrice is the row''s dual, the change in cost per unit of the row''s right-hand side, and the RVP row is in index units. The value of relief is per whole psi and positive when relief saves money.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'In SPEC_TEMPLATES, which limits does the Gasoline, 10 ppm sulfur template set?', '["RON 95, MON 85, sulfur 10 ppm and RVP 8.5 psi, with density 0.72 to 0.775 kg/l.", "RON 95, MON 85 and sulfur 10 ppm, with RVP kept at 9 psi and density 0.72 to 0.775 kg/l.", "RON 91, MON 81 and sulfur 10 ppm, with RVP 8.5 psi and density 0.72 to 0.775 kg/l.", "RON 95, MON 85, sulfur 10 ppm and RVP 8.5 psi, with density 0.82 to 0.845 kg/l."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 10 ppm template carries RON 95, MON 85, Sulfur 10 ppm and RVP 8.5 psi; the 50 ppm one carries 91, 81, 50 and 9. Both carry Density on volume from 0.72 to 0.775 kg/l. Templates are starting points. They are not a compliance source.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'The AGO cargo''s cetane minimum of 48 binds. What is one cetane number of relief worth, and which way does the relief run?', '["1159.3909 $ per unit, by raising the cetane minimum above 48.", "1159.3909 $ per unit, by lowering the cetane minimum below 48.", "0.1932 $ per unit, the rowPrice, by lowering the minimum.", "852453.4687 $ per unit, the density figure, which the cetane row shares."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Relief is raising a maximum or lowering a minimum, and the price is the money saved by one unit of it. The Cetane number minimum prints rowPrice 0.1932, scale 6000.0000 and price 1159.3909 per unit.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'Suppose the Apapa sulfur row had been built with volume weights in place of SG weights. Where would the digest''s two-route check show it?', '["Nowhere: both routes read the same row, so they share any error made in building it.", "As an infeasible status for the whole pool, reported by the kernel''s phase one.", "As a difference other than 0.0000 on the Sulfur line.", "As a refusal naming the sulfur row, printed where the engine''s refusals are listed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'propertyOfBlend recomputes a property from a finished recipe by the specification''s own rule, separately from the LP rows, so agreement is a check. Both routes print 50.0000 for Sulfur, with a difference of 0.0000.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'Moving the RVP limit to 10 prints a saving of 3651.3942 $, and moving it to 8 prints -4751.5037 $, around 4448.9659 $ per psi. Which causes does the digest name for that spread?', '["Rounding in the rowPrice of -0.2569, printed to four decimals and so drifting over a psi.", "The Butane bound, which the re-solves remove before moving the limit.", "The Density range, which binds once the RVP limit moves a whole psi.", "The optimum moves to a different vertex, and the RVP row''s index units are not a straight line in psi."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The RVP re-solves change what holds (at 10 psi only Sulfur binds; at 8 psi Reformate sits at its availability), so the optimum moves to a different vertex, and the RVP row is in index units, which are not a straight line in psi. Rounding is not among the causes the digest names.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'What does the Apapa recipe achieve for MON, and what does it give away?', '["84.4928, a giveaway of 3.4928.", "94.5010, a giveaway of 3.5010.", "84.4928, a giveaway of 3.5010.", "84.4928, a giveaway of 0.0000, binding."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Apapa table prints MON achieved 84.4928 against its minimum of 81, giveaway 3.4928, binding false. 94.5010 and 3.5010 are RON''s.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'At Apapa the RVP rowPrice times the 8000.0000 bbl batch prints -2054.8893. In what unit is that figure?', '["$ per psi, the value of one psi of RVP relief at the limit", "$ per ppm, the unit the sulfur row''s figure is in", "$ per index point, as the row is in index units", "$ per bbl, the cost of one more barrel"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest prints: rowPrice x 8000.0000 bbl is -2054.8893 $ per index point. The RVP row is in index units.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'How does solveLP carry a tank limit such as Butane''s 400 bbl at Apapa?', '["As an extra row, Butane''s barrels at most 400, priced like a specification.", "As an upper bound on Butane''s volume in lo <= x <= hi, with no extra row for it.", "As a term in the objective that charges Butane more near its tank''s limit.", "As a limit in the template, beside the Sulfur and RVP maximums."]'::jsonb, 1, 'solveLP keeps bounds as bounds, lo <= x <= hi, and does not add them as rows. The result reports a component on its upper bound in its own line: "Components at their availability: Butane."'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'Compare a component whose cost is blank with one whose sulfur figure is blank. How does the engine treat each?', '["Both are refused: the engine never solves with a blank in the table, and names the component.", "Both are read as zero, so the component is free in one case and sulfur-free in the other.", "Both are skipped: the recipe solves without the component that carries the blank.", "The blank cost is refused; the blank sulfur skips that one specification and the recipe solves."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The blank cost returns the refusal that begins "No cost for Isomerate". The blank sulfur figure returns optimal at 695245.0644 $, with Sulfur listed as skipped and its reason.'),
  ('crude', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'The digest counts the LP oracle''s golden cases as 181 problems. How does its method stand against the kernel''s absolute tolerances?', '["The kernel''s own absolute tolerances, shared by running a simplex with Bland''s rule.", "A larger tolerance than the kernel''s, sized for problems scaled in millions.", "It works in exact rational arithmetic, with no simplex at all.", "A relative tolerance on each pivot, set tighter than the kernel''s absolute ones."]'::jsonb, 2, 'oracle_lp.py uses exact rational vertex enumeration with no simplex at all. The kernel''s absolute tolerances are what held item L4 is about.');
